  "Number": 7,
  "Question": "If \\(x\\) is a real number, then the quantity \\((1-|x|)(1+x)\\) is positive if and only if",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(|x|\\lt1\\)",
    "B": "\\(x\\lt1\\)",
    "C": "\\(|x|\\gt1\\)",
    "D": "\\(x\\lt-1\\)",
    "E": "\\(x\\lt-1\\) or \\(-1\\lt x\\lt1\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "A product is positive exactly when its factors have the same nonzero sign. Here the signs can change at \\(x=-1\\) and \\(x=1\\), where a factor is zero.\n\nIf \\(x<-1\\), then \\(|x|>1\\). Thus \\(1-|x|<0\\) and \\(1+x<0\\), so the product is positive.\n\nIf \\(-1<x<1\\), then \\(|x|<1\\). Both \\(1-|x|\\) and \\(1+x\\) are positive, so their product is positive.\n\nIf \\(x>1\\), then \\(1-|x|<0\\) while \\(1+x>0\\), so the product is negative. At \\(x=-1\\) and \\(x=1\\), the product is zero and is therefore excluded.\n\nThese cases cover all real numbers. The quantity is positive exactly when \\(x<-1\\) or \\(-1<x<1\\), choice E.",
